# MECHANISM PIN — a converse alias must be spelled so the reader can see it is one.
#
# Fixture INLINE (mechanism pin): the subject is the ENGINE's corpus discipline, not any
# particular knowledge base.
#
# THE TRAP THIS CLOSES. `bilga` had two spellings: `obliged(bound, duty)` and a converted
# alias `obligated` whose arguments were EXCHANGED. In English "obligated" and "obliged"
# are synonyms — same participle, same meaning — so nothing in the surface form told a
# hand-writer that one inverted the other. A consuming constitution states
# `obliged(Review, $x)`, "the review body owes a duty about this person"; written with the
# other spelling it silently meant THE PERSON OWES THE DUTY TO THE REVIEW BODY — the exact
# inversion of the guarantee the rule exists to make. The only thing between that and a
# green suite was a hand-written two-pin discriminator.
#
# The alias is now `obligated_by`, following `metabolized_by` — the `_by` suffix is the
# corpus's own marker that arguments run the other way. `obligated(...)` no longer
# resolves, so the typo is a COMPILE ERROR rather than a wrong answer.
#
# Note the other three converse aliases were already self-marking: `owned`/`owns`,
# `permitted`/`permits` and `metabolized_by`/`cuts` each differ morphologically between
# passive and active. `obligated`/`obliged` was the only pair where both spellings read
# active, which is why it alone was a trap.

# ── THE CONVERSE STILL HOLDS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
obliged(Review, Ruk).

? obliged(Review, Ruk).
# => TRUE

# The discriminator the consuming constitution relies on: direction is load-bearing.
? obliged(Ruk, Review).
# => FALSE

# `obligated_by` is the SAME fact with the arguments exchanged — that is what a converse
# alias means, and it must keep meaning it.
? obligated_by(Ruk, Review).
# => TRUE

? obligated_by(Review, Ruk).
# => FALSE

# ── THE INVERTING SPELLING IS GONE ───────────────────────────────────────────
# Not "discouraged", not "linted" — unresolvable. A KB that writes the old spelling fails
# to compile instead of quietly asserting the opposite of what it says.
:refuse syntax /unknown predicate "obligated"/
obligated(Review, Ruk).

# ── THE DEONTIC FORM ROUTES THE SAME WAY ─────────────────────────────────────
# (The shipped corpora write these duties with the PLAIN `obliged` spelling, so that the
# bound party lands on x1 where the corpus places put it — see the 2026-08-17 migration.
# The alias must keep working, and keep inverting, which is what this section pins.)
# `obligated_by(every X, event { P() })` is the DEONTIC form of the alias. The swap
# applies here too — pinned because the deontic path is a different code path and could
# regress independently of the plain relational one.
person(Ruk).
:accept
obligated_by(every person, event { eats() }).

? obligated_by(Ruk, event { eats() }).
# => TRUE

? obliged(Ruk, event { eats() }).
# => FALSE

:expect-pins 8
